{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Atlanta Braves announced themselves early and emphatically. A blistering April — 19 wins against just 8 losses — set the tone for what looked like a dominant season. Matt Olson was crushing baseballs into the right-field seats, Ozzie Albies was providing the kind of timely power that makes a lineup dangerous, and Dominic Smith's clutch double in mid-April ignited a team that looked poised to run away with the division. By the end of May the Braves sat among baseball's elite, riding a wave of homers and timely hitting that had the Truist Park faithful dreaming of October glory.\n\nJune brought the first real turbulence. A 9-15 month exposed cracks that the gaudy April numbers had papered over. Raisel Iglesias became a recurring villain in the team's worst moments — a walk-off surrender in early June and another brutal blown save in early July that kept opponents alive in games Atlanta had no business losing. Yet for all the bullpen heartbreak, the offense kept the Braves competitive, and the underlying run differential remained firmly positive. Michael Harris II was quietly having a breakout season, and Ozzie Albies delivered the most electric swing of the summer — a two-run blast on June 20th that rescued a game the Braves desperately needed.\n\nJuly and August were the Braves at their most resilient. Back-to-back 16- and 17-win months steadied a ship that had briefly listed in June, fueled by a deeper lineup than most opponents anticipated. Ha-Seong Kim provided veteran savvy, Drake Baldwin emerged as a surprise contributor, and Michael Harris II's late-August double was the kind of highlight-reel play that reminded everyone why Atlanta's ceiling was so high. Ronald Acuña Jr.'s grand slam in September — his 20th homer — punctuated a stretch run that got the Braves into the postseason with 94 wins and genuine momentum.\n\nThe playoffs, though, told a harsher story. Atlanta survived a tense Wild Card series against the Phillies — Austin Riley's three-run homer in Game 1 was the signature moment of October, and the team clawed through two hard-fought games — before running headlong into the Dodgers in the NLDS. Robert Suarez and the Los Angeles bullpen neutralized Atlanta's offense, and the Braves' own relievers couldn't hold the fort when it mattered most. A season full of promise ended one round short, leaving a talented roster to wonder what might have been — and to carry the hunger of a near-miss into 2027.",
